1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,900
We'll add a filter to the capture machine and compare raw and smoothed readings.

2
00:00:05,900 --> 00:00:10,220
Use the same project and wiring. Paste this folder's two files and save.

3
00:00:11,220 --> 00:00:16,180
Most of this file is already awake. It's the capture machine from demo one.

4
00:00:16,680 --> 00:00:23,720
Line 107 is new: decimation 16, meaning we print every sixteenth sample.

5
00:00:24,220 --> 00:00:29,660
Line 119 makes room for filtered results. It starts full of zeros.

6
00:00:30,660 --> 00:00:34,660
Lines 139 to 142 connect timer to converter.

7
00:00:35,160 --> 00:00:43,060
Lines 207 to 217 swap buffers. Notice the filter is not run here. Handlers stay

8
00:00:43,060 --> 00:00:43,560
fast.

9
00:00:44,560 --> 00:00:48,160
Switch on lines 263 to 265.

10
00:00:48,660 --> 00:00:50,980
Line 263: eight taps.

11
00:00:51,480 --> 00:00:58,904
Line 265 lists eight weights, mirrored around the middle. Together they equal 32768,

12
00:00:58,904 --> 00:01:00,760
which stands for one.

13
00:01:01,760 --> 00:01:05,120
Switch on lines 316 to 342.

14
00:01:05,620 --> 00:01:10,660
Line 316 remembers seven samples from the previous buffer.

15
00:01:11,160 --> 00:01:16,680
Line 318: the function takes raw input, an output buffer, and a count.

16
00:01:17,180 --> 00:01:23,420
Line 323 visits every sample. Line 325 starts a total at zero.

17
00:01:24,420 --> 00:01:27,140
Line 327 visits all eight taps.

18
00:01:27,640 --> 00:01:34,680
Line 329 finds how far back to look: this position minus the tap number.

19
00:01:35,180 --> 00:01:42,396
Line 330 chooses. If that position is zero or more, use this buffer. Otherwise use

20
00:01:42,396 --> 00:01:43,100
history.

21
00:01:43,600 --> 00:01:47,600
Line 332 multiplies weight by sample and adds to the total.

22
00:01:48,100 --> 00:01:55,220
Line 335 shifts right by fifteen, dividing by 32768.

23
00:01:55,720 --> 00:01:59,080
If every sample were 1000, what would come out?

24
00:01:59,580 --> 00:02:04,580
Your turn. Pause the video.

25
00:02:04,580 --> 00:02:07,460
1000, because the weights add to one.

26
00:02:08,460 --> 00:02:15,180
Lines 338 to 341 save the last seven raw samples for the next buffer.

27
00:02:16,180 --> 00:02:22,772
Build and load now. The second number is still zero, because nobody calls the

28
00:02:22,772 --> 00:02:25,940
function. These numbers are examples.

29
00:02:26,940 --> 00:02:33,020
Switch on line 418, inside the main loop. It filters the finished buffer.

30
00:02:33,520 --> 00:02:39,520
Lines 421 to 424 print every sixteenth pair: raw, then filtered.

31
00:02:40,020 --> 00:02:45,700
Build and load. The second number comes alive. These are examples.

32
00:02:46,700 --> 00:02:51,371
Install two Python helpers, close your terminal, then run plot underscore fir

33
00:02:51,371 --> 00:02:53,980
underscore demo dot py with your port name.

34
00:02:54,480 --> 00:02:56,640
Turn the knob and compare the two lines.

35
00:02:57,640 --> 00:03:03,532
Try eight equal weights of 4096 on line 265. Which version smooths more? Compare

36
00:03:03,532 --> 00:03:06,920
them on the graph, then restore the originals.

37
00:03:07,420 --> 00:03:11,820
On line 107, try four. Can the text line keep up?

38
00:03:12,320 --> 00:03:17,320
Your turn. Pause the video.

39
00:03:17,320 --> 00:03:20,440
Four times as many lines may be too much for it.

40
00:03:20,940 --> 00:03:26,620
You now understand a digital filter, one line at a time. Wonderful work.

